1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,325
This is STAT 331, video 12. The topic is random vectors and the multivariate normal distribution, from Lecture 5.

2
00:00:10,775 --> 00:00:17,239
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:17,689 --> 00:00:28,676
Type A: compute the mean and the variance of one linear combination, a transpose y. Practice question 1 of Lecture 5 and Tutorial 3 ask this.

4
00:00:29,126 --> 00:00:39,430
Type B: compute the mean vector and the covariance matrix of A y, a vector of linear combinations. Practice question 1 asks this too.

5
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:50,973
Type C: prove a rule for a mean or a covariance matrix with matrix algebra. The slides prove two rules, and practice question 2 asks for a third.

6
00:00:51,423 --> 00:01:04,735
Type D: find the distribution of a linear function of a multivariate normal vector, and decide if a vector is multivariate normal. The slides use it for multiple linear regression.

7
00:01:05,185 --> 00:01:20,353
Section 1 defines the two summaries that all four question types use. In multiple linear regression, we have n responses, y 1 to y n. We stack them in one column. This column is a random vector.

8
00:01:20,803 --> 00:01:26,457
Each response is a random variable. So each entry has its own distribution.

9
00:01:26,907 --> 00:01:34,203
The first summary is the mean vector: the column of the means of the entries. The purple dots mark the means.

10
00:01:34,653 --> 00:01:42,375
The second summary is the covariance matrix. It shows how each entry varies, and how the entries vary together.

11
00:01:42,825 --> 00:01:54,303
Let y be a random vector with entries y 1 to y n. The T means transpose, so y is a column. Each curve shows the distribution of one entry.

12
00:01:54,753 --> 00:02:00,790
Each entry has an expected value. The purple dot marks the mean of each distribution.

13
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:09,261
The mean of the vector is the column of these means. We take the expectation entry by entry. We call this vector mu.

14
00:02:09,711 --> 00:02:22,490
The variance of a random vector is the expectation of y minus mu, times its transpose. y minus mu is a column, n by 1. Its transpose is a row, 1 by n.

15
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:37,319
A column times a row gives an n by n matrix. Entry 1, 2 comes from y 1 minus mu 1, times y 2 minus mu 2. The matrix holds the expectation of each such product.

16
00:02:37,769 --> 00:02:44,617
On the diagonal, i equals j. There the expectation of the square is the variance of y i.

17
00:02:45,067 --> 00:03:01,088
Off the diagonal, the expectation of the product is the covariance of y i and y j. For example, entry 1, 2 is the covariance of y 1 and y 2. We call this matrix V, the covariance matrix.

18
00:03:01,538 --> 00:03:13,314
V is symmetric. Entry 1, 3 equals entry 3, 1, because the covariance of y 1 and y 3 equals the covariance of y 3 and y 1.

19
00:03:13,764 --> 00:03:31,044
Here are samples of a random vector with two entries. Each dot is one draw of y 1 and y 2. Here V has variances 1 and covariance 0.8. A large y 1 comes with a large y 2, so the cloud tilts up.

20
00:03:31,494 --> 00:03:40,305
Here the covariance is 0. The cloud has no tilt. The green ellipse is a circle, because the two variances are equal.

21
00:03:40,755 --> 00:03:49,352
Here the covariance is minus 0.8. A large y 1 comes with a small y 2, so the cloud tilts down.

22
00:03:49,802 --> 00:03:58,122
So the diagonal of V gives the spread of each entry. All three clouds have variances 1, so they have the same spread.

23
00:03:58,572 --> 00:04:03,564
The off-diagonal entries give the tilt: how the two entries move together.

24
00:04:04,014 --> 00:04:15,129
The slides give one more property. V is positive semi-definite: for every vector a in R n, a transpose V a is greater than or equal to 0.

25
00:04:15,579 --> 00:04:19,760
Here is the reason. Pick a direction a. The purple arrow shows it.

26
00:04:20,210 --> 00:04:30,535
Project each point onto the line. The projected value is a transpose y, one number for each draw. The orange histogram shows these numbers.

27
00:04:30,985 --> 00:04:44,916
Section 2 shows that the variance of a transpose y is a transpose V a. A variance is never negative. So a transpose V a is never negative, for every a. Type A uses this as a check.

28
00:04:45,366 --> 00:05:01,558
Section 2 is about one linear combination. Slide 26: z is the sum of a i y i, plus a constant c. In matrix form, z is a transpose y plus c. A row times a column gives one number.

29
00:05:02,008 --> 00:05:12,675
Take the expectation. The expectation of a sum is the sum of the expectations, and constants come out. So E of z is a transpose mu, plus c.

30
00:05:13,125 --> 00:05:33,903
Let u be a second combination, b transpose y plus d. Slide 26 recalls the covariance: a double sum of a i, times b j, times the covariance of y i and y j. The grid has one cell for each pair. The marked cell is i equals 1, j equals 2.

31
00:05:34,353 --> 00:05:45,532
The covariance of y i and y j is entry i, j of V. So the grid is V, with the weight a i on row i and b j on column j.

32
00:05:45,982 --> 00:05:55,667
Add all n squared cells. In matrix form, this sum is a transpose V b. The constants c and d do not change a covariance.

33
00:05:56,117 --> 00:06:10,389
Now take u equal to z, so b equals a. The covariance of z with itself is its variance. So the variance of a transpose y is a transpose V a. The sizes give 1 by 1: one number.

34
00:06:10,839 --> 00:06:29,165
These are the rules of slides 27 to 29. E of a constant vector is the vector itself. E of a transpose y plus b is a transpose E of y, plus b. The variance of a transpose y is a transpose V a. Type A uses them.

35
00:06:29,615 --> 00:06:44,057
Question type A asks for the mean and the variance of one linear combination, a transpose y. Practice question 1 of Lecture 5 asks it with numbers. Tutorial 3 asks it with computer output.

36
00:06:44,507 --> 00:06:54,662
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: read the vector a, the mean vector mu, and the covariance matrix V. Check that the sizes match.

37
00:06:55,112 --> 00:07:02,963
Step 2: the mean. E of a transpose y is a transpose mu. Multiply each a i by mu i, and add.

38
00:07:03,413 --> 00:07:08,682
Step 3: compute V a, a column. Entry i is row i of V times a.

39
00:07:09,132 --> 00:07:17,260
Step 4: the variance is a transpose times V a. Multiply each a i by entry i of V a, and add.

40
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:25,326
Step 5: check that the variance is not negative. If the question asks for a standard error, take the square root.

41
00:07:25,776 --> 00:07:43,163
Here is practice question 1 of Lecture 5. Pause the video and try it. y has three entries, with mean 1, 2, 2, and the covariance matrix V on the right. a is 1, 2, 3. Step 1 of card A: read mu, a and V. All have size 3.

42
00:07:43,613 --> 00:07:52,829
Step 2: E of a transpose y is a transpose mu. 1 times 1, plus 2 times 2, plus 3 times 2, is 11.

43
00:07:53,279 --> 00:08:03,476
Step 3: V a. Row 1 of V is 4, 0, 1. Times a: 4 times 1, plus 0 times 2, plus 1 times 3, is 7.

44
00:08:03,926 --> 00:08:11,499
Row 2 of V is 0, 3, minus 1. 0, plus 3 times 2, minus 1 times 3, is 3.

45
00:08:11,949 --> 00:08:21,336
Row 3 of V is 1, minus 1, 4. 1, minus 2, plus 4 times 3, is 11. So V a is 7, 3, 11.

46
00:08:21,786 --> 00:08:33,541
Step 4: a transpose times V a. 1 times 7, plus 2 times 3, plus 3 times 11. That is 7 plus 6 plus 33, which is 46.

47
00:08:33,991 --> 00:08:45,340
Step 5: 46 is positive, so the check passes. The answers: E of a transpose y is 11, and the variance of a transpose y is 46.

48
00:08:45,790 --> 00:09:08,723
Now a type A question with computer output: Tutorial 3, question 1 d. Pause the video and try it. The estimates form a random vector, beta hat, and R gives its covariance matrix, v cov. Step 1: beta 2 hat minus beta 1 hat is a transpose beta hat, with a equal to 0, minus 1, 1.

49
00:09:09,173 --> 00:09:21,696
The estimate is a transpose beta hat: minus 3527.1, plus 6035.2. This is 2508.1.

50
00:09:22,146 --> 00:09:34,754
Step 3: V a. Row 1 of v cov times a: 0, plus 32012.55, minus 32012.55. This is 0.

51
00:09:35,204 --> 00:09:50,649
Row 2: 0, minus 136994.86, plus 32012.55. This is minus 104982.31.

52
00:09:51,099 --> 00:10:06,779
Row 3: 0, minus 32012.55, plus 241977.18. This is 209964.63.

53
00:10:07,229 --> 00:10:26,472
Step 4: a transpose times V a. 0, plus 104982.31, plus 209964.63. The variance is 314946.94.

54
00:10:26,922 --> 00:10:44,095
Step 5: the variance is positive. Its square root, the standard error, is 561.2. The tutorial solution uses this standard error in the 95 percent confidence interval. A later video covers that step.

55
00:10:44,545 --> 00:10:59,799
Section 3 is about many linear combinations at once. Slide 30: z 1 is a 1 1 y 1, plus a 1 2 y 2, and so on, to a 1 n y n. The same holds for z 2 to z k.

56
00:11:00,249 --> 00:11:06,201
Collect the weights in a k by n matrix A. Row 1 of A times y gives z 1.

57
00:11:06,651 --> 00:11:17,659
Each row of A gives one entry of z. So the k equations are one matrix equation: z equals A y. z is a random vector with k entries.

58
00:11:18,109 --> 00:11:31,037
Take the expectation of each entry. E of z i is the sum over j of a i j times E of y j. Stack the k results: E of z equals A times E of y.

59
00:11:31,487 --> 00:11:44,969
Slide 31 also gives the variance: the variance of z equals A, times the variance of y, times A transpose. Section 4 proves both rules. Type B uses them.

60
00:11:45,419 --> 00:11:55,766
Question type B asks for the mean vector and the covariance matrix of A y. Practice question 1 asks it. Method card B has five steps.

61
00:11:56,216 --> 00:12:06,499
Step 1: the sizes. A is k by n, and y is n by 1. So E of A y is k by 1, and the variance of A y is k by k.

62
00:12:06,949 --> 00:12:12,069
Step 2: E of A y is A mu. Entry i is row i of A times mu.

63
00:12:12,519 --> 00:12:24,828
Step 3: compute A V first. It is k by n. Row i of A V is row i of A times V. This is a weighted sum of the rows of V, with the weights in row i of A.

64
00:12:25,278 --> 00:12:36,755
Step 4: multiply A V by A transpose. Column j of A transpose is row j of A. So entry i, j is row i of A V times row j of A.

65
00:12:37,205 --> 00:12:45,867
Step 5: check. A covariance matrix is symmetric, and its diagonal entries are variances, so they are not negative.

66
00:12:46,317 --> 00:12:58,925
Back to practice question 1. Now we use card B. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: A is 4 by 3, and mu is 3 by 1. So E of A y is 4 by 1.

67
00:12:59,375 --> 00:13:08,932
Step 2: E of A y is A mu. Row 1 of A is 1, minus 1, 0. Times mu: 1, minus 2, plus 0, is minus 1.

68
00:13:09,382 --> 00:13:15,462
Row 2 of A is 0, 1, minus 1. 0, plus 2, minus 2, is 0.

69
00:13:15,912 --> 00:13:22,483
Row 3 of A is 1, 0, minus 1. 1, plus 0, minus 2, is minus 1.

70
00:13:22,933 --> 00:13:27,839
Row 4 of A is 1, 1, 1. 1, plus 2, plus 2, is 5.

71
00:13:28,289 --> 00:13:32,407
So E of A y is minus 1, 0, minus 1, 5.

72
00:13:32,857 --> 00:13:50,435
Now the variance of A y. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card B: 4 by 3, times 3 by 3, times 3 by 4, gives 4 by 4. A V is 4 by 3. We call the rows of V v 1, v 2 and v 3.

73
00:13:50,885 --> 00:14:06,267
Step 3: compute A V, row by row. Row 1 of A is 1, minus 1, 0. So row 1 of A V is v 1 minus v 2: 4, 0, 1, minus 0, 3, minus 1. This is 4, minus 3, 2.

74
00:14:06,717 --> 00:14:16,274
Row 2 of A is 0, 1, minus 1. So row 2 of A V is v 2 minus v 3. This is minus 1, 4, minus 5.

75
00:14:16,724 --> 00:14:23,871
Row 3 of A is 1, 0, minus 1. v 1 minus v 3 is 3, 1, minus 3.

76
00:14:24,321 --> 00:14:32,043
Row 4 of A is 1, 1, 1. The sum of the three rows of V is 5, 2, 4. Now A V is complete.

77
00:14:32,493 --> 00:14:46,936
Step 4 of card B: entry i, j is row i of A V times row j of A. Entry 1, 1: 4, minus 3, 2, times 1, minus 1, 0. 4, plus 3, plus 0, is 7.

78
00:14:47,386 --> 00:14:56,303
Entry 2, 2: minus 1, 4, minus 5, times 0, 1, minus 1. 0, plus 4, plus 5, is 9.

79
00:14:56,753 --> 00:15:05,073
Entry 3, 3: 3, 1, minus 3, times 1, 0, minus 1. 3, plus 0, plus 3, is 6.

80
00:15:05,523 --> 00:15:12,734
Entry 4, 4: 5, 2, 4, times 1, 1, 1. 5, plus 2, plus 4, is 11.

81
00:15:13,184 --> 00:15:23,424
Now the entries above the diagonal. Entry 1, 2: row 1 of A V times row 2 of A. 0, minus 3, minus 2, is minus 5.

82
00:15:23,874 --> 00:15:30,594
Entry 1, 3: row 1 of A V times row 3 of A. 4, plus 0, minus 2, is 2.

83
00:15:31,044 --> 00:15:37,849
Entry 1, 4: row 1 of A V times row 4 of A. 4, minus 3, plus 2, is 3.

84
00:15:38,299 --> 00:15:45,702
Entry 2, 3: row 2 of A V times row 3 of A. Minus 1, plus 0, plus 5, is 4.

85
00:15:46,152 --> 00:15:54,067
Entry 2, 4: row 2 of A V times row 4 of A. Minus 1, plus 4, minus 5, is minus 2.

86
00:15:54,517 --> 00:16:01,514
Entry 3, 4: row 3 of A V times row 4 of A. 3, plus 1, minus 3, is 1.

87
00:16:01,964 --> 00:16:22,679
Step 5. V is symmetric, so A V A transpose is symmetric too. The entries below the diagonal are mirror copies: for example, entry 2, 1 equals entry 1, 2, minus 5. The diagonal entries, 7, 9, 6 and 11, are positive, as variances must be.

88
00:16:23,129 --> 00:16:36,419
Section 4 proves the rules. Question type C asks you to prove a rule for a mean or a covariance matrix. The slides prove two rules, and practice question 2 asks for a third.

89
00:16:36,869 --> 00:16:49,392
Method card C. Step 1: write the definition. A mean is taken entry by entry. A variance is the expectation of u minus its mean, times the transpose.

90
00:16:49,842 --> 00:16:55,986
Step 2: centre with the mean rule. A y minus its mean is A times y minus mu.

91
00:16:56,436 --> 00:17:05,033
Step 3: the transpose of a product reverses the order. A w, transpose, is w transpose, A transpose.

92
00:17:05,483 --> 00:17:16,470
Step 4: take the constants out of the expectation. A stays on the left, and A transpose stays on the right, because the order of a matrix product matters.

93
00:17:16,920 --> 00:17:22,424
Step 5: name the known part in the middle, here V, and check that the sizes match.

94
00:17:22,874 --> 00:17:36,613
Here is the proof of slides 32 and 33. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: write A y entry by entry. Entry 1 is the sum over i of a 1 i y i.

95
00:17:37,063 --> 00:17:45,361
The expectation of a vector is taken entry by entry. On the right is one entry, j, inside the expectation.

96
00:17:45,811 --> 00:17:55,881
Step 4: the expectation of a sum is the sum of the expectations, and the constants a come out. So each term becomes a times E of y i.

97
00:17:56,331 --> 00:18:05,568
Step 5: name the result. Entry j is row j of A times the column E of y. So the column is A times E of y.

98
00:18:06,018 --> 00:18:13,741
So E of A y equals A E of y. Type B uses it in step 2, and card C uses it in step 2.

99
00:18:14,191 --> 00:18:32,687
Now the variance rule, slides 34 and 35. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card C: write the definition of the variance, with A y in place of y. The picture shows the sizes: a column times a row gives a k by k matrix.

100
00:18:33,137 --> 00:18:43,185
Step 2: by the mean rule, A y minus E of A y equals A, times y minus E of y. A comes out on the left in both factors.

101
00:18:43,635 --> 00:18:54,152
Step 3: the transpose of a product reverses the order. So the second factor becomes y minus E of y, transpose, times A transpose.

102
00:18:54,602 --> 00:19:05,738
Step 4: A and A transpose are constants. A comes out on the left, and A transpose comes out on the right. The expectation acts only on the random middle part.

103
00:19:06,188 --> 00:19:19,543
Step 5: the middle part is the definition of the variance of y. So the variance of A y is A, times the variance of y, times A transpose. The result is k by k.

104
00:19:19,993 --> 00:19:28,078
This is the variance rule of slide 31. Type B uses it in steps 3 and 4, and type D uses it too.

105
00:19:28,528 --> 00:19:49,776
Practice question 2. Pause the video and try it. x has mean mu and variance V. y is A x, and w is B x. Entry i, j of C is the covariance of y i and w j. In the variance matrix of y and w stacked, C is the top right block, p by q.

106
00:19:50,226 --> 00:20:06,397
Step 1 of card C: write the definition. C is the expectation of y minus its mean, times w minus its mean, transpose. Entry i, j of this matrix is the covariance of y i and w j.

107
00:20:06,847 --> 00:20:12,863
Step 2: centre with the mean rule. y minus E of y is A times x minus mu.

108
00:20:13,313 --> 00:20:18,646
In the same way, w minus E of w is B times x minus mu.

109
00:20:19,096 --> 00:20:21,400
Put lines 2 and 3 into line 1.

110
00:20:21,850 --> 00:20:29,637
Step 3: the transpose of B times x minus mu is x minus mu, transpose, times B transpose.

111
00:20:30,087 --> 00:20:38,300
Step 4: A and B transpose are constants. They come out of the expectation, A on the left and B transpose on the right.

112
00:20:38,750 --> 00:20:50,633
Step 5: the middle part is V. So C equals A V B transpose. The sizes check: p by n, times n by n, times n by q, gives p by q.

113
00:20:51,083 --> 00:20:56,608
The answer does not contain mu. A shift of the mean does not change a covariance.

114
00:20:57,058 --> 00:21:10,434
Section 5 is about the multivariate normal distribution. Start with z, a vector of n independent standard normal variables. Its cloud is round and centred at 0.

115
00:21:10,884 --> 00:21:32,068
Slide 36: multiply z by a constant matrix A, and add a constant vector mu. Then y equals A z plus mu has a multivariate normal distribution. We write: y follows M V N, mu, Sigma. In the example, A stretches and turns the cloud, and mu moves its centre to 2, 1.

116
00:21:32,518 --> 00:21:37,809
Find the mean with the mean rule. E of y equals A times E of z, plus mu.

117
00:21:38,259 --> 00:21:46,323
Each z i has mean 0, so E of z is the zero vector. So E of y is mu: the centre of the cloud.

118
00:21:46,773 --> 00:21:59,295
Find the variance with the variance rule. Adding the constant mu does not change the variance. So the variance of y is A, times the variance of z, times A transpose.

119
00:21:59,745 --> 00:22:27,756
The entries of z are independent with variance 1, so the variance of z is the identity matrix I. So the variance of y is A A transpose. We call it Sigma. In the example: 1.2 squared is 1.44, 1.2 times 0.9 is 1.08, and 0.9 squared plus 0.6 squared is 1.17. Sigma sets the shape of the ellipse.

120
00:22:28,206 --> 00:22:36,761
So y is M V N with mean mu and variance Sigma equal to A A transpose. Type D uses this definition.

121
00:22:37,211 --> 00:22:47,536
Slide 36 also gives the density of y. The bars around Sigma mean its determinant. The density is highest at mu, the purple point.

122
00:22:47,986 --> 00:23:03,986
The density depends on y only through one number: the quadratic form, y minus mu, transpose, Sigma inverse, y minus mu. On each green ellipse, this quadratic form is constant, so the density is constant.

123
00:23:04,436 --> 00:23:09,855
The outer ellipses have a larger quadratic form. There the density is smaller.

124
00:23:10,305 --> 00:23:21,121
Check with n equal to 1. Sigma is the number sigma squared. The square root of its determinant is sigma, and its inverse is 1 over sigma squared.

125
00:23:21,571 --> 00:23:26,776
The quadratic form becomes y minus mu, squared, over sigma squared.

126
00:23:27,226 --> 00:23:39,301
So the density is 1 over the square root of 2 pi sigma squared, times e to the minus y minus mu squared over 2 sigma squared. This is the normal density.

127
00:23:39,751 --> 00:23:50,076
The slides give four properties of the multivariate normal. Property 1 is linearity. Start from the definition: y equals A z plus mu.

128
00:23:50,526 --> 00:23:59,507
Let u equal C y plus d, with a constant matrix C and a constant vector d. Put in y equals A z plus mu.

129
00:23:59,957 --> 00:24:14,592
Multiply out. u equals C A times z, plus C mu plus d. This is a constant matrix times z, plus a constant vector: the same form as the definition. So u is multivariate normal.

130
00:24:15,042 --> 00:24:19,159
The mean: E of z is 0, so E of u is C mu plus d.

131
00:24:19,609 --> 00:24:33,220
The variance: C A times its transpose. The transpose of C A is A transpose C transpose. In the middle, A A transpose is Sigma. So the variance of u is C Sigma C transpose.

132
00:24:33,670 --> 00:24:43,462
So u is M V N with mean C mu plus d and variance C Sigma C transpose. Type D uses this in steps 2 to 4.

133
00:24:43,912 --> 00:24:55,709
Property 2 is about marginal distributions. Take a subset of the entries of y, for example only y 1. Then this subset is also multivariate normal.

134
00:24:56,159 --> 00:25:05,141
The bars show the values of y 1 alone, from the same cloud. The green curve is a normal density, and it fits the bars.

135
00:25:05,591 --> 00:25:16,087
The reason is linearity. To pick entries, multiply by a matrix of zeros and ones. Here y 1 equals the row 1, 0, times y.

136
00:25:16,537 --> 00:25:32,046
Property 3 is about conditional distributions. Split y into two parts, y 1 and y 2, and fix y 2 at one value. Here we keep only the points with y 2 near 1.2, in the orange band.

137
00:25:32,496 --> 00:25:45,445
The distribution of y 1 among these points is normal again. So y 1 given y 2 is multivariate normal. The slides do not give the formulas for its mean and variance.

138
00:25:45,895 --> 00:26:02,087
Property 4 is about independence. Let y be multivariate normal. If Sigma i j is 0, then y i and y j are independent. Here Sigma 1 2 is 0, and the ellipse lies along the axes.

139
00:26:02,537 --> 00:26:10,047
Here Sigma 1 2 is 0.7. The ellipse tilts, and y 1 and y 2 depend on each other.

140
00:26:10,497 --> 00:26:20,459
This property needs a multivariate normal vector. The exercise at the end of this section shows a vector that is not multivariate normal.

141
00:26:20,909 --> 00:26:34,413
Question type D asks for the distribution of a linear function of a multivariate normal vector, or asks if a vector is multivariate normal. Method card D has five steps.

142
00:26:34,863 --> 00:26:48,965
Step 1: check that the vector is multivariate normal. Either the question says so, or the vector is A z plus mu with independent standard normal z. Entries that are each normal are not enough.

143
00:26:49,415 --> 00:26:54,193
Step 2: write the new vector as C y plus d, and name C and d.

144
00:26:54,643 --> 00:26:57,075
Step 3: the mean is C mu plus d.

145
00:26:57,525 --> 00:27:05,909
Step 4: the variance is C Sigma C transpose. By linearity, u is M V N with this mean and variance.

146
00:27:06,359 --> 00:27:17,282
Step 5: read off what the question asks. Each entry is normal, with its mean and its diagonal variance. A zero covariance gives independence.

147
00:27:17,732 --> 00:27:40,601
Now the reason for this lecture: multiple linear regression, slide 45. Pause the video and try it. Stack the n equations. y is n by 1. X is n by p plus 1, with a first column of ones. Beta is p plus 1 by 1. Epsilon is n by 1. So y equals X beta plus epsilon.

148
00:27:41,051 --> 00:28:06,843
Step 1 of card D: is epsilon multivariate normal? The errors are independent normals with mean 0 and variance sigma squared. So epsilon equals sigma times z, with independent standard normal z. This is the definition with A equal to sigma I. Its variance matrix has sigma squared on the diagonal and 0 everywhere else: sigma squared I.

149
00:28:07,293 --> 00:28:17,363
Step 2: y equals the identity times epsilon, plus X beta. X and beta are constants. So C is I, and d is X beta.

150
00:28:17,813 --> 00:28:22,911
Step 3: the mean is I times 0, plus X beta, which is X beta.

151
00:28:23,361 --> 00:28:36,503
Step 4: the variance is I, times sigma squared I, times I transpose, which is sigma squared I. So y is M V N with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I.

152
00:28:36,953 --> 00:28:53,657
Step 5: read off. Each y i is normal with mean x i transpose beta, where x i transpose is row i of X, and variance sigma squared. The off-diagonal entries are 0, so the y i are independent.

153
00:28:54,107 --> 00:29:10,448
This is slide 45: y equals X beta plus epsilon, with epsilon M V N 0, sigma squared I, is the same as y M V N, X beta, sigma squared I. The next videos use this model.

154
00:29:10,898 --> 00:29:30,034
A type D problem with the practice question 1 data. This part is added; it is not on the slides. Pause the video and try it. Suppose y is multivariate normal with the same mean and covariance matrix. Step 1 of card D: y is given as M V N.

155
00:29:30,484 --> 00:29:40,681
First, a transpose y. Step 2: it is C y plus d, with C equal to a transpose, the row 1, 2, 3, and d equal to 0.

156
00:29:41,131 --> 00:29:46,571
Step 3: the mean is a transpose mu. The type A example found it: 11.

157
00:29:47,021 --> 00:29:57,325
Step 4: the variance is a transpose V a, which is 46. So a transpose y is normal with mean 11 and variance 46.

158
00:29:57,775 --> 00:30:12,431
Now y 1 alone. C is the row 1, 0, 0. C mu picks mu 1, which is 1. C V C transpose picks V 1 1, which is 4. So y 1 is normal with mean 1 and variance 4.

159
00:30:12,881 --> 00:30:21,073
Step 5: V 1 2 is 0. y is M V N, so y 1 and y 2 are independent, by property 4.

160
00:30:21,523 --> 00:30:35,091
Now the exercise of the slides. Pause the video and try it. X 1 is standard normal. The blue histogram shows a sample of X 1, and the red curve is the N 0 1 density.

161
00:30:35,541 --> 00:30:50,005
B is Bernoulli with probability one half, independent of X 1. If B is 0, X 2 equals X 1. If B is 1, X 2 equals minus X 1. So we flip the sign half of the time.

162
00:30:50,455 --> 00:31:01,293
The normal density is symmetric about 0. So a flip of the sign does not change the distribution. The green histogram of X 2 has the same shape.

163
00:31:01,743 --> 00:31:13,412
The red curve fits X 2 too. So X 2 is standard normal. The slides check this with a simulation of one million draws. Here we use 4000 draws.

164
00:31:13,862 --> 00:31:26,555
Part 2 of the exercise. Let Y equal X 1 plus X 2. Is Y normal? And is the vector X 1, X 2 multivariate normal? Pause the video and try it.

165
00:31:27,005 --> 00:31:35,219
Step 1 of card D: is the vector multivariate normal? Look at the pairs X 1, X 2 from the simulation.

166
00:31:35,669 --> 00:31:44,906
When B is 0, X 2 equals X 1. These are the blue points, on the line with slope 1. Then Y equals 2 X 1.

167
00:31:45,356 --> 00:31:57,964
When B is 1, X 2 equals minus X 1. These are the green points, on the line with slope minus 1. Then Y equals X 1 minus X 1, which is 0.

168
00:31:58,414 --> 00:32:11,833
So the probability that Y equals 0 is the probability that B equals 1, which is one half. A normal variable has probability 0 at any single value. So Y is not normal.

169
00:32:12,283 --> 00:32:32,635
So the vector X 1, X 2 is not multivariate normal. If it were, Y, the row 1, 1 times the vector, would be normal by linearity. An M V N cloud fills an ellipse, and this cloud is two lines. The answer of step 1 is no, so the M V N rules do not apply.

170
00:32:33,085 --> 00:32:43,815
One more fact. X 2 equals 1 minus 2 B, times X 1: when B is 0 this is X 1, and when B is 1 it is minus X 1.

171
00:32:44,265 --> 00:32:57,727
B is independent of X 1. So the covariance of X 1 and X 2 is E of X 1 squared, times E of 1 minus 2 B. This is 1 times 0, which is 0.

172
00:32:58,177 --> 00:33:11,617
But X 1 and X 2 are not independent: the absolute value of X 2 always equals the absolute value of X 1. So property 4 needs a multivariate normal vector.

173
00:33:12,067 --> 00:33:32,099
To recap. A random vector has a mean vector and a covariance matrix. The covariance matrix is symmetric and positive semi-definite. Card A: for a transpose y, the mean is a transpose mu, and the variance is a transpose V a: first V a, then a transpose times it.

174
00:33:32,549 --> 00:33:43,813
Card B: for A y, the mean is A mu, and the variance is A V A transpose: first A V, then times A transpose. Check that the result is symmetric.

175
00:33:44,263 --> 00:33:54,311
Card C proves such rules: write the definition, centre with the mean rule, transpose, take the constants out of the expectation, and name V.

176
00:33:54,761 --> 00:34:19,529
Card D: A z plus mu is multivariate normal. C y plus d is M V N with mean C mu plus d and variance C Sigma C transpose. Check first that the vector is M V N. In multiple linear regression, y is M V N with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I. The next video estimates beta by least squares.
